## Proof

**Answer: Yes.** Every invertible $f:\mathbb{R}^4\to\mathbb{R}^4$ mapping straight lines to straight lines while preserving constancy of velocity is necessarily affine, $f(x)=Ax+b$.

---

**Setup.** Let $f:\mathbb{R}^4\to\mathbb{R}^4$ be invertible, mapping every line to a line and preserving constancy of velocity. Set $b:=f(0)$ and $F(x):=f(x)-f(0)$, so $F(0)=0$. Since translating the output by a constant preserves both hypotheses and the conclusion, it suffices to prove $F$ is linear.

**Key consequence of "preserving constancy of velocity."** A line through $p$ in direction $v\neq 0$ is $\gamma(t)=p+tv$, traversed at constant velocity $v$. Its image is a line traversed at constant velocity in the same parameter $t$, so there exist $q,u$ with
$$f(p+tv)=q+tu,\quad \forall\,t\in\mathbb{R}.$$
Setting $t=0$ gives $q=f(p)$, hence
$$f(p+tv)=f(p)+t\,u,\quad u=f(p+v)-f(p)\ \text{(set }t=1\text{)}.$$
Therefore, for all $p,v\in\mathbb{R}^4$ and all $t\in\mathbb{R}$:
$$\boxed{f(p+tv)=(1-t)\,f(p)+t\,f(p+v).}\tag{$\ast$}$$
Since $F=f-f(0)$ and $F(0)=0$, the identical relation holds for $F$:
$$F(p+tv)=(1-t)F(p)+t\,F(p+v).\tag{$\ast\!F$}$$

Note $u=f(p+v)-f(p)\neq 0$ whenever $v\neq 0$ (else $f$ would be constant on a line, contradicting injectivity), so the image of every line is a **full** line.

**Step 1 — Homogeneity: $F(\lambda x)=\lambda F(x)$.** Take $p=0$, $v=x$ in $(\ast F)$:
$$F(tx)=F(0)+t\bigl(F(x)-F(0)\bigr)=t\,F(x),\quad\forall\,t\in\mathbb{R}.\qquad\checkmark$$

**Step 2 — Additivity: $F(x+y)=F(x)+F(y)$.** The four points $0,\,x,\,y,\,x+y$ form a (possibly degenerate) parallelogram whose diagonals $[0,x+y]$ and $[x,y]$ share the midpoint $m:=\tfrac{x+y}{2}$.

- The point $m$ lies on the line through $0$ and $x+y$; applying $(\ast F)$ with $p=0$, $v=x+y$, $t=\tfrac12$:
$$F(m)=\tfrac12 F(x+y).\tag{1}$$
- The point $m$ lies on the line through $x$ and $y$; applying $(\ast F)$ with $p=x$, $v=y-x$, $t=\tfrac12$ (so $p+tv=x+\tfrac12(y-x)=m$):
$$F(m)=\tfrac12 F(x)+\tfrac12 F(y).\tag{2}$$

Equating (1) and (2): $\tfrac12 F(x+y)=\tfrac12\bigl(F(x)+F(y)\bigr)$, hence
$$F(x+y)=F(x)+F(y).\qquad\checkmark$$

(When $x,y$ are linearly dependent, additivity already follows from Step 1; the parallelogram argument covers the independent case, so the proof is complete in all cases.)

**Step 3 — Conclusion.** Steps 1 and 2 show $F:\mathbb{R}^4\to\mathbb{R}^4$ is $\mathbb{R}$-linear, so $F(x)=Ax$ for a unique $4\times 4$ matrix $A$. Therefore
$$f(x)=Ax+b,\qquad b=f(0).$$
Since $f$ is invertible, $A$ is invertible. $\blacksquare$

**Remark on the role of hypotheses.** The "constancy of velocity" condition yields the exact affine-parameter preservation $(\ast)$, from which linearity follows by the parallelogram/midpoint argument (using $\dim\geq 2$ for the independent case). Mere line-to-line bijectivity already forces affinity over $\mathbb{R}$ by the fundamental theorem of affine geometry (since $\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb{R})=\{\mathrm{id}\}$); the velocity condition makes the proof self-contained and strengthens the conclusion to hold even without invoking field-automorphism theory.

